# Review tour frame validation

Some source contracts deliberately require more records than a tour displays.
The bilateral hamstring contract checks eight muscle surfaces and both femurs,
although an individual sequence displays four same-side muscles and one femur.

Review packets contain that visible five-record subset. It must not replace
the complete trusted catalogue when the parser recomputes camera frames:
doing so fails bilateral source admission and rejects genuine worksheets.

`validTours` first compares the complete received tour packet, including source
records, bundles, frame data and captions, with independently generated trusted
evidence. It then recomputes step frames using the same trusted full catalogue.
The existing per-structure, bundle, identity, tour, reference, source and display
integrity checks remain in place. No source contract or packet equality check
is relaxed, and response-owned geometry cannot become trusted camera context.

The first regression run also exposed a separate transport gap: the parser
checked the shape, but not the value, of `materialHash`. It now recomputes all
three worksheet fingerprints and the material hash after display-pin admission,
using the generator's unchanged canonical JSON and original hash domains. Missing,
extra or forged fingerprints are refused. Native WebCrypto failure stays closed;
no full teaching corpus, fallback hash, approval or new hash domain is introduced.

`scripts/test-body-review-tour-context.mjs` exercises actual JSON worksheets for
all ten hamstring contexts, a retained non-hamstring worksheet, altered packets
and refusal of missing or changed contralateral source records. Review packets
remain unsubmitted drafts; successful parsing does not grant approval, access,
spatial registration or permission to publish patient data.
